  <section>
    <title>Self-authenticating BACAP payload</title>
    <para>The first message sent, the <code>VoucherPayload</code>, is authenticated in the following
      manner: </para>
    <itemizedlist spacing="compact">
      <listitem>
        <para>The <code>VoucherPayload</code> is computed (first). </para>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
        <para>A cryptographic hash of the <code>VoucherPayload</code> is computed. This hash
            <emphasis role="strong">is</emphasis> the <code>Voucher</code>. </para>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
        <para>The <code>Voucher</code> is then used to derive a BACAP read/write capability set. </para>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
        <para>The <code>VoucherPayload</code> is uploaded to the sequence described by the
          capability (at index 0). </para>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
        <para>Anyone who intercepts the <code>Voucher</code> can read  from <emphasis role="strong"
            >and</emphasis> write to the message sequence. </para>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
        <para>But: Since the <code>Voucher</code> is a hash over the <code>VoucherPayload</code>,
          writing the sequence with anything but the <code>VoucherPayload</code> will be detectable
          by the recipient. </para>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
        <para>This means that the contents <emphasis>cannot</emphasis> be undetectably modified by
          the interceptor. </para>
      </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
  </section>
